"Find a planet around another star from the two signals it makes: the small dip in the star’s light when the planet passes in front, and the star’s small wobble as the planet pulls it around. From them, work out the planet’s size, its mass, its orbit, what it is made of, and whether it could have liquid water.",
"<strong>We almost never see the planet itself.</strong> A planet is billions of times fainter than its star and, from light years away, sits right beside it. So astronomers watch the star instead.",
"<strong>Transits measure size.</strong> If we see the orbit edge on, the planet passes in front of the star once every orbit and blocks a little of its light. The share of light blocked is about the planet’s area over the star’s area: (planet radius ÷ star radius) squared. A star’s middle is a little brighter than its edge, so the dip at the middle of a transit is about {#} times that ratio. Brightness drops are given in parts per million (ppm): {#} {#} ppm is {#}%. The Sun’s radius is {#} Earth radii.",
"<strong>The wobble measures mass.</strong> The planet does not simply orbit the star: both go around their shared center of mass. The star swings toward us and away from us once each orbit, and its light shifts slightly blue, then red (the Doppler effect). The top speed of the star, \\(K\\), is bigger for a heavier planet and for a closer one. Earth moves the Sun at only {#} m/s ({#} cm/s, a slow crawl), Jupiter at about {#} m/s.",
"<strong>Recipes.</strong> For a planet that transits, on a nearly circular orbit, and much lighter than its star (all true here), with the star’s mass \\(M_s\\) in Sun masses and the period \\(P\\) in years:",
"Planet radius in Earth radii = {#} × star radius (in Sun radii) × √(brightness drop ÷ {#}), with the drop as a fraction (ppm ÷ {#} {#} {#}).",
"Planet mass in Earth masses = (\\(K\\) ÷ {#} m/s) × \\(P^{1/3}\\) × \\(M_s^{2/3}\\). (On a calculator, \\(x^{1/3}\\) is the cube root and \\(x^{2/3}\\) is the cube root of \\(x^2\\).)",
"Orbit size in AU, from Kepler’s third law: \\(a = (M_s \\times P^2)^{1/3}\\).",
"Density in g/cm³ = {#} × mass ÷ radius³, with mass and radius in Earth units. Earth is {#}, rock about {#} to {#}, water {#}, Jupiter {#}, Saturn {#}.",
"<strong>Could it have liquid water?</strong> The starlight a planet gets, compared with Earth, is the star’s luminosity \\(L\\) (its power, Sun = {#}) divided by \\(a^2\\). For stars near the Sun’s mass a rough rule is \\(L \\approx M_s^4\\). A planet like Earth could keep liquid water if it gets between about {#} and {#} times Earth’s starlight: that is the habitable zone (Kopparapu and others, {#}, for a Sun like star). With no greenhouse and an albedo like Earth’s, a planet’s temperature is about {#} K × (starlight compared with Earth)\\(^{1/4}\\).",
"Jupiter’s radius is {#} Earth radii. What fraction of the Sun’s light does Jupiter block when it crosses the Sun? What fraction does Earth block? Give both in ppm.",
